---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import DocPage from '../../components/docs/DocPage.astro';
import ApiTable from '../../components/docs/ApiTable.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { SRC_URL, PAPERS_URL } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Quantum causal models — DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'The per-node Choi–Jamiołkowski factorization, the quantum Markov condition enforced as a freeze-time commutativity check with a condition-driven tolerance, and C₃-exclusion faithfulness derived from the frozen graph.';

const setup = `use deep_causality_quantum::{
    CommutatorTolerance, FactorSupports, ProcessFactors, freeze_quantum,
};

// The factors are freeze-time decoration, keyed by graph node index.
let mut factors = ProcessFactors::<FloatType>::new();
factors.insert(0, sigma_x());
factors.insert(1, sigma_z());

// Their Hilbert supports. Both live on leg 0, so the supports intersect.
let mut supports = FactorSupports::new();
supports.declare(0, &[0]);
supports.declare(1, &[0]);

let report = freeze_quantum(
    &mut graph,
    &[],                              // state writers
    &factors,
    &supports,
    &CommutatorTolerance::default(),
    None,                             // no faithfulness check this call
)?;`;

const faithful = `// Declaring input and output systems turns on the second gate.
let report = freeze_quantum(
    &mut graph,
    &[],
    &factors,
    &supports,
    &CommutatorTolerance::default(),
    Some((&inputs, &outputs)),
)?;`;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/qcm/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <DocPage
    eyebrow="Layer · Model"
    title="Quantum causal models"
    lede="Channels where the conditional probability tables used to be, plus the condition that decides whether a product of them is a model at all."
    sourceHref={`${SRC_URL}/types/qcm`}
    sourceLabel="src/types/qcm"
  >
    <h2>The problem</h2>

    <p>
      A classical causal model factorizes a joint distribution over its graph:
      each variable contributes a conditional probability table given its
      parents, and the product of those tables is the joint. Nothing constrains
      the tables beyond being distributions. Any product of them is a model.
    </p>

    <p>
      The quantum version replaces each table with an operator. Node
      <code>Aᵢ</code> carries a Choi–Jamiołkowski operator
      <code>ρ&#123;Aᵢ|Pa(Aᵢ)&#125;</code>, and the model is the product
      <code>σ = ∏ᵢ ρ&#123;Aᵢ|Pa(Aᵢ)&#125;</code>, called the process operator.
      Operators do not commute in general, so the product now depends on the
      order in which it is taken, and the model is only well defined when that
      dependence goes away.
    </p>

    <p>
      Lorenz (2022, Def. 3.3) states the condition. Factors whose Hilbert
      supports intersect must pairwise commute. Factors on disjoint supports are
      unconstrained, because operators on separate tensor legs commute for free.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the factors live</h2>

    <p>
      The factors are not carried on the runtime state channel. They ride an
      external <code>ProcessFactors</code> store, keyed by graph node index,
      with a companion <code>FactorSupports</code> recording which tensor legs
      each factor acts on and how wide each leg is. That store is the operator
      analogue of the engine's edge-keyed lambda store, and it is static
      freeze-time data.
    </p>

    <p>
      Keeping it static is what makes the condition checkable once. If the
      factors could change while the graph runs, every step would have to
      recheck, and the cost of the check would scale with the run rather than
      with the model.
    </p>

    <Code code={setup} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      <code>FactorSupports::from_graph</code> derives the same layout from the
      graph instead, which removes the chance of the declared supports and the
      graph disagreeing. Either way, <code>validate</code> runs before anything
      else, so a shape error is reported as a shape error rather than as a
      downstream freeze failure.
    </p>

    <h2>The check</h2>

    <p>
      <code>quantum_markov_check</code> walks factor pairs. A pair with disjoint
      supports is skipped and never appears in the report, because it carries no
      obligation. For a pair that does intersect, both factors are embedded onto
      the union of their legs by <code>embed_on_legs</code>, the commutator is
      formed there by <code>matrix_commutator</code>, and its Frobenius norm is
      compared against a threshold.
    </p>

    <p>The threshold is a first-order forward-error budget:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        ‖[ρⱼ, ρₖ]‖_F ≤ C · (‖ρⱼ‖·budgetₖ + ‖ρₖ‖·budgetⱼ + 2·γₙ·‖ρⱼ‖·‖ρₖ‖),
        with γₙ = n·u / (1 − n·u)
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Here <code>n</code> is the dimension of the embedded operators,
      <code>u</code> is the unit roundoff, and <code>C</code> is a safety
      factor. The defaults are <code>C = 8</code>, <code>u = ε</code>, and a
      per-node budget of <code>u·‖ρ‖_F</code>. A model whose factors were built
      through a long chain of operations can hand a larger budget for that one
      node to <code>with_budget</code>. Naming the node is more honest than
      loosening the safety factor for every pair at once.
    </p>

    <p>
      Note that the threshold is condition-driven rather than linear in graph
      depth. The quantity that grows is the norm of the operators involved, not
      the number of nodes between them.
    </p>

    <p>
      The check is sound and may be incomplete. It never accepts a pair whose
      commutator exceeds the threshold; it does not claim to reject every pair
      that a sharper analysis would reject.
    </p>

    <h2>What a failure looks like</h2>

    <p>
      A failing pair produces
      <code>QuantumErrorEnum::CommutatorNonZero &#123; node_j, node_k, detail
      &#125;</code>. The two node indices are fields rather than text, so a
      caller can act on them.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>freeze_quantum</code> runs the check as the level-specific hook
      inside the engine's own freeze. That hook can only return the engine's
      error type, which would ordinarily flatten the structured error into a
      message. The crate stashes the typed error and recovers it after the
      rollback, so the caller reads the variant rather than parsing a string.
      A failure in the engine's built-in checks, such as acyclicity or the
      single-writer rule, surfaces instead as
      <code>QuantumErrorEnum::CalculationError</code> carrying the graph error's
      message.
    </p>

    <p>
      On any failure the graph rolls back to its dynamic state. There is no
      half-frozen outcome.
    </p>

    <h2>Faithfulness</h2>

    <p>
      Passing declared input and output systems adds a second gate after the
      commutativity check.
    </p>

    <Code code={faithful} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      <code>CausalStructure::from_graph_reachability</code> reads a bipartite
      influence relation off the now-frozen graph: input <code>i</code> relates
      to output <code>o</code> when <code>o</code> is reachable from
      <code>i</code>. The relation is then tested for the C₃ obstruction of van
      der Lugt and Lorenz (arXiv:2508.11762, Thm 3.2).
    </p>

    <p>
      C₃ is the bipartite six-cycle: <code>K₃,₃</code> minus a perfect matching.
      In a 3×3 induced block, every input relates to exactly two of the three
      outputs and every output to exactly two of the three inputs. Canonically it
      is two commuting CNOTs. A structure containing it provably has no
      traditional-circuit causally faithful decomposition, so
      <code>find_c3</code> searches every 3×3 block and
      <code>check_c3_exclusion</code> rejects the model with
      <code>NotFaithfullyRepresentable</code>, naming the three inputs and three
      outputs that carry the obstruction.
    </p>

    <p>
      Faithfulness claims stop there. The general routed and direct-sum
      hypothesis of Lorenz and Barrett is open upstream, and the crate does not
      speak to it.
    </p>

    <h2>What the report carries</h2>

    <p>
      On success <code>freeze_quantum</code> returns a
      <code>QuantumMarkovReport</code>: one <code>CommutatorCheck</code> per
      tested pair, each recording the two node indices, the commutator norm, the
      threshold it was compared against, the ratio of the two, and the verdict.
      <code>tested_pairs()</code> gives the count and
      <code>worst_margin()</code> the largest ratio seen. A margin at or below 1
      passes, so the worst margin is the number to watch when a model is close
      to its tolerance.
    </p>

    <h2>Scope</h2>

    <p>
      The crate supports flat models. Quantum-subgraph nesting is not supported:
      the physical meaning of such nesting is itself unestablished, so more work
      needs to be done.
    </p>

    <p>
      This whole layer sits behind the <code>qcm</code> feature, which is on by
      default. It pulls in <code>deep_causality</code> for the causal graph, and
      that crate is std-only, so <code>qcm</code> implies <code>std</code> and is
      off in a bare-metal build. The papers behind the layer are committed in
      the crate: see <a href={PAPERS_URL}>papers/</a> and the
      <a href="/papers/">papers page</a>.
    </p>
  </DocPage>

  <div class="wide-wrap">
    <ApiTable layer="qcm" title="The qcm surface" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wide-wrap {
    max-width: var(--w-panel);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-4) var(--space-9);
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) { .wide-wrap { padding: 0 var(--space-5) var(--space-9); } }
</style>
